  <h2 id="intro">Everyone Wants to Talk About Distribution</h2>

  <div class="soup" aria-label="Common distribution advice">
    <b>Post more on LinkedIn</b><b>Get active on Reddit</b><b>Make Reels</b><b>Start a YouTube channel</b><b>Send more emails</b><b>Be everywhere</b>
  </div>

  <p>There's no shortage of advice telling brands where to distribute content, and most of it isn't wrong. Distribution matters.</p>

  <p>But a step usually gets skipped.</p>

  <p class="pull">What exactly are you distributing?</p>

  <p>If your content strategy starts with figuring out what to post on LinkedIn tomorrow, you're solving the wrong problem. Before you worry about distribution, you need something worth distributing, something useful, something authoritative, something your company actually knows, something that answers a real question your customer is asking. And ideally, something that lives somewhere you control.</p>

  <p><strong>Your website should be the knowledge base. Distribution is how those ideas travel.</strong></p>

  <section class="chunk" id="rented">
    <div class="sec-label"><i>1</i> The Problem <span></span></div>
    <h2>Brands Are Building on Rented Land</h2>

    <p>Social platforms are powerful. They're where people spend time, where ideas spread, and where customers discover brands they didn't know existed. But there's an important distinction between your website and most distribution channels.</p>

    <div class="srcs">
      <div class="src">
        <h4><span>&#10003;</span>You own</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Your website and everything on it</li>
          <li>Your structure, internal links, and data</li>
          <li>Your relationship with the customer</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="src">
        <h4><span>&#10005;</span>You don't own</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>LinkedIn, Instagram, Facebook, TikTok</li>
          <li>Reddit, YouTube, and their algorithms</li>
          <li>The formats, the rules, or your reach</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>Those platforms control the algorithms, the formats, the rules, and ultimately your access to the audience. That doesn't mean you shouldn't use them, you absolutely should. It means they should play a specific role in your content system.</p>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <span class="tag">Takeaway</span>
      <p>They're distribution channels. They shouldn't be your entire knowledge base.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>The disposable content treadmill</h3>

    <p>Imagine your marketing strategy starts with this question every Monday: "What should we post this week?"</p>

    <p>You brainstorm. Someone writes a LinkedIn post. Someone makes an Instagram graphic. Someone records a short video. Someone writes an email. You publish everything. Then Monday comes around again.</p>

    <p>That's the treadmill. You're creating disposable pieces instead of building something that compounds. A LinkedIn post might perform well for a few days. An email might generate clicks. A video might reach a new audience. Those are valuable outcomes, but what did you build? Where can someone go six months later to find the information?</p>

    <div class="warnbox">
      <p>If every idea lives only inside individual distribution channels, <strong>your knowledge is fragmented across platforms you don't control.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chunk" id="knowledge">
    <div class="sec-label"><i>2</i> The Asset <span></span></div>
    <h2>Your Website Should Be Your Knowledge Base</h2>

    <p>This requires changing how you think about your website. It isn't just a homepage, an About page, product pages, and a checkout. It should increasingly become the knowledge base for what your company knows. Ask yourself:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>If your company has solved a particular problem for 10 years, where does that knowledge live?</li>
      <li>If your sales team has answered the same question 500 times, where does that answer live?</li>
      <li>If you've tested five approaches and know why three don't work, where is that documented?</li>
      <li>If customers constantly misunderstand something about your category, have you explained it?</li>
      <li>If your product works especially well for a specific use case, have you documented why?</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If the answer is "our team knows that," it isn't enough. Your company's expertise has to become accessible information.</p>

    <p class="pull">Customers can't discover knowledge that only exists inside your company. Neither can machines.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="chunk" id="answer">
    <div class="sec-label"><i>3</i> The Starting Point <span></span></div>
    <h2>Start With the Authoritative Answer</h2>

    <p>This connects directly to how we think about content at VEWO: find the questions, answer them, repeat. But there's an important next step.</p>

    <p>Once you find a good question, don't immediately ask what your LinkedIn post should say. First ask: <strong>what is the best answer we can create?</strong></p>

    <p>Build that answer. Make it useful and specific. Include your experience and evidence. Add examples. Answer the obvious follow-ups. Explain the nuances. Then publish it somewhere you control. Now you have an authoritative source, and distribution becomes much easier.</p>

    <h3>One great answer becomes an entire distribution system</h3>

    <p>Say you sell premium outdoor furniture, and customers repeatedly ask: <em>"What's the best outdoor furniture material for extreme heat?"</em></p>

    <p>Instead of creating seven unrelated social posts this week, answer that question exceptionally well on your website: how heat affects different materials, aluminum versus teak versus steel versus synthetics, what fades, what needs maintenance, what matters in desert climates, the mistakes buyers make, and what your company has learned firsthand.</p>

    <figure>
      <div class="fig-frame">
        <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 1</p>
        <p class="fig-title">One authoritative answer, six native expressions</p>
        <div class="grid6">
          <div class="g6"><b>LinkedIn</b><span>"The most expensive outdoor furniture isn't always the best choice for extreme heat. Here's what actually matters…"</span></div>
          <div class="g6"><b>Instagram</b><span>A carousel: 5 things to consider before buying patio furniture for extreme heat</span></div>
          <div class="g6"><b>YouTube</b><span>Aluminum vs. teak, which holds up better in hot climates?</span></div>
          <div class="g6"><b>Email</b><span>One takeaway: before you buy outdoor furniture this summer, check these three things</span></div>
          <div class="g6"><b>Reddit</b><span>Genuine participation in a thread about materials for hot climates, information, not a link drop</span></div>
          <div class="g6"><b>Short-form video</b><span>30 seconds on why some patio furniture becomes unusable in direct summer sun</span></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Different formats, different audiences, different platforms. One underlying piece of expertise.</figcaption>
      </div>
    </figure>
  </section>

