              <text x="496" y="168">Email · Video · Communities</text>
            </g>

            <g font-family="-apple-system,Segoe UI,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="12.5" fill="#6b7480" text-anchor="middle">
              <text x="487" y="232">Some of that audience, and the AI systems helping them,</text>
              <text x="487" y="249">come back to the deeper source</text>
            </g>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <figcaption>The website becomes the source. The channels become the distribution network. Then you repeat the process with the next important question.</figcaption>
      </div>
    </figure>

    <h3>Distribution amplifies authority. It doesn't create it.</h3>

    <p>Imagine someone posting 20 times a week about a topic they barely understand. They're distributing a lot, but that doesn't make the underlying information authoritative.</p>

    <p>Now imagine a company with years of category experience, proprietary testing, customer data, product expertise, and a genuine point of view. That company already possesses something valuable: knowledge. The opportunity is to turn it into accessible information and then distribute it.</p>

    <div class="srcs">
      <div class="src">
        <h4><span>1</span>Builds an asset</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Starts from what your company knows</li>
          <li>Publishes the answer where you control it</li>
          <li>Compounds, it's still working in a year</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="src">
        <h4><span>2</span>Feeds a treadmill</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Starts from what the algorithm rewards</li>
          <li>Lives only on platforms you rent</li>
          <li>Resets every Monday</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chunk" id="ai">
    <div class="sec-label"><i>5</i> The AI Angle <span></span></div>
    <h2>Why This Matters More in the Age of AI</h2>

    <p>This isn't only a social media strategy. It's increasingly an AI visibility strategy.</p>

    <p>AI systems need information. They need to understand who your company is, what you know, what problems you solve, what topics you're associated with, who your products are right for, how you compare with alternatives, and whether other sources reinforce those associations.</p>

    <p>Your website is where you establish that. Distribution helps those ideas move beyond it, discussed, shared, referenced, mentioned, expanded on, creating a broader information footprint around your brand.</p>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <span class="tag">Takeaway</span>
      <p>Your website and your distribution channels shouldn't compete. They should reinforce one another.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>SEO, AEO, GEO, and distribution should work together</h3>

    <p>It's easy to turn these into separate initiatives. SEO over here. Social over there. Email somewhere else. PR doing its own thing. Someone experimenting with GEO. But customers don't experience your company in departments, and neither does the internet. One authoritative answer creates multiple outcomes.</p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>What the same asset does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>SEO</td><td>Ranks for relevant searches and long-tail questions</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AEO</td><td>Clear structure makes specific answers easier to identify and surface</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GEO</td><td>Helps generative AI understand your brand and potentially reference your expertise</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Social</td><td>The ideas generate conversation and discovery</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Email</td><td>Educates your existing audience</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Video</td><td>Reaches people who prefer watching to reading</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Earned media</td><td>Original insight or testing becomes something publications want to reference</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>These shouldn't be seven unrelated content strategies. They're seven ways of creating value from the same knowledge.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="chunk" id="native">
    <div class="sec-label"><i>6</i> The Craft <span></span></div>
    <h2>Distribution Isn't Copy and Paste</h2>

    <p>One important caveat. Repurposing doesn't mean pasting the first three paragraphs of your blog onto every platform.</p>

    <p>Each channel has its own culture. What works on LinkedIn might not work on Reddit. What works in an email might not work on Instagram. A 10-minute YouTube video won't work as a 30-second Reel.</p>

    <div class="flowset">
      <div class="flowrow now">
        <span class="rowlabel">The rule</span>
        <div class="steps steps1"><em>The idea travels. The format changes.</em></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>On LinkedIn, lead with an insight. On Reddit, participate in a discussion and answer a specific question. On Instagram, make it visual. On YouTube, make it a demonstration. In email, focus on one takeaway and link to the deeper explanation.</p>

    <p><strong>Same expertise, native execution.</strong> That's far stronger than blasting identical content everywhere.</p>

    <h3>Stop creating content from scratch for every channel</h3>

    <p>This is also an operational problem. If your social, email, video, and website teams are all independently inventing content, you're creating unnecessary work. Everyone needs ideas, research, expertise, and source material. Instead, create a common foundation.</p>

    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>1</b><span>Question worth answering</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>1</b><span>Authoritative answer on your site</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>6+</b><span>Native expressions your team never has to invent</span></div>
    </div>

    <p>Now your social person isn't starting with a blank page. Neither is your email marketer or your video team. Your sales team has something to send. Your support team has something to reference. Your whole organization works from the same body of knowledge, which is more efficient, and creates a far more consistent story around your brand.</p>

    <h3>What content is worth distributing?</h3>

    <p>Not every article deserves a ten-channel campaign. Prioritize content with something worth amplifying:</p>

    <div class="grid6">
      <div class="g6"><b>Real question</b><span>If nobody cares about the question, distribution won't fix that</span></div>
      <div class="g6"><b>Something to say</b><span>Generic content doesn't improve because you post it seven times</span></div>
      <div class="g6"><b>Demonstrates expertise</b><span>Your strongest content shows what your company actually knows</span></div>
      <div class="g6"><b>Connects to the business</b><span>No hard sell needed, but it should reinforce where you want authority</span></div>
      <div class="g6"><b>Multiple conversations</b><span>Strong pieces contain several ideas worth extracting separately</span></div>
      <div class="g6"><b>Still true later</b><span>Evergreen answers keep earning; news cycles don't</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chunk" id="flywheel">
    <div class="sec-label"><i>7</i> The System <span></span></div>
    <h2>The Content Flywheel</h2>

    <p>Done properly, this becomes a flywheel rather than a treadmill.</p>

    <figure>
      <div class="fig-frame">
        <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 3</p>
        <p class="fig-title">Listen, answer, distribute, discover, learn, improve</p>
        <div class="os">
          <div class="os-track">
            <div class="os-step"><div class="os-num">1</div><div><h4>Listen</h4><p>What customers are asking</p></div></div>
            <div class="os-step"><div class="os-num">2</div><div><h4>Answer</h4><p>Authoritatively, on your site</p></div></div>
            <div class="os-step"><div class="os-num">3</div><div><h4>Distribute</h4><p>Native formats per channel</p></div></div>
            <div class="os-step"><div class="os-num">4</div><div><h4>Discover</h4><p>New audiences meet the idea</p></div></div>
            <div class="os-step"><div class="os-num">5</div><div><h4>Learn</h4><p>What they engage with or question</p></div></div>
            <div class="os-step"><div class="os-num">6</div><div><h4>Improve</h4><p>Strengthen it, find the next question</p></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="os-repeat">↻ &nbsp;Distribution sits in the middle, never at the beginning</div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>You need something valuable before you can amplify it. That single ordering is the difference between a flywheel and a treadmill.</figcaption>
      </div>
    </figure>

    <h3>What should you measure?</h3>

    <p>Don't judge this system on social engagement alone. Likes and views are useful indicators, not the objective.</p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table class="prep">
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What to watch</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Website</td><td>Organic impressions, rankings, traffic, engagement, internal navigation, conversions</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Distribution</td><td>Reach, engagement, shares, comments, video views, email clicks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Authority</td><td>Backlinks, brand mentions, citations, earned media, community discussion</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AI visibility</td><td>Brand mentions in relevant AI answers, citation visibility, accuracy of brand descriptions, competitive presence, questions where you appear or stay absent</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>The goal isn't more content, and it isn't more distribution. It's a system where useful information creates increasing visibility and authority over time.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="chunk" id="faq">
    <div class="sec-label"><i>8</i> Questions <span></span></div>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div class="faq">
      <details open>
        <summary>Should we stop posting on social until our website content is strong?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>No. Keep the channels active, but change what feeds them. Start sourcing posts from authoritative answers you publish on your own site instead of inventing something new each week.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How many channels should one article support?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>As many as your customers genuinely use, and no more. A strong foundational piece can usually support three to six native formats. Ten channels executed poorly is worse than three done natively.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Isn't this just repurposing content?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>Repurposing usually means reformatting the same text. This is different: the website answer is the source of record, and each channel gets a native expression of the idea rather than a copy of the paragraphs.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does distribution help AI visibility, or only search?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>Both. Your site establishes what you know; off-site discussion, mentions, and references reinforce it. AI systems cross-check the wider web, so a brand talked about consistently across platforms is easier to understand and recommend.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What if we don't have anything unique to say yet?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>You almost certainly do, it's just trapped in sales calls, support tickets, and product decisions. Start there: the questions your team answers repeatedly are your first authoritative articles.</p></div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chunk" id="conclusion">
    <div class="sec-label"><i>9</i> Conclusion <span></span></div>
    <h2>Build the Source Before You Build the Megaphone</h2>

    <p>Distribution matters. LinkedIn, Reddit, YouTube, Instagram, email, and communities all matter. But none of them eliminate the need for something valuable underneath. Without strong source material, adding channels just creates more places where your team has to invent something to say. That's exhausting.</p>

    <p>Instead: find a question worth answering. Create the best answer you can. Put the authoritative version on your website. Turn the ideas inside it into content for the channels where your customers spend time. Let those channels amplify your expertise. Learn from the response. Then answer the next question.</p>

    <p>Because before you ask where you should distribute your content, ask a more important question: <strong>do we have something worth distributing?</strong></p>

    <p>Build that first. Then amplify it.</p>

    <p class="pull">Distribution amplifies authority. It doesn't create it.</p>
  </section>

</div>

